abef554=3D"" class=3D"marky"><div class=3D"md-container"><p>The case titled=
 "Laura Alcaraz and Laura Elisa Rodriguez vs. Los Angeles County Metropolit=
an Transportation Authority" was filed in the Superior Court of California,=
 County of Los Angeles, on September 21, 2020. The presiding judge is Krist=
in Escalante, and the case number is 20STCV36044. This complaint falls unde=
r the category of personal injury, property damage, and wrongful death, spe=
cifically focusing on allegations of negligence related to a motor vehicle =
incident.</p><p>The plaintiffs in this case are Laura Alcaraz, an individua=
l, and Laura Elisa Rodriguez, represented by her guardian ad litem, Laura A=
lcaraz. They are represented by attorney Harry Nalbandyan from Levin &amp; =
Nalbandyan, LLP. The defendant is the Los Angeles County Metropolitan Trans=
portation Authority, a public entity.</p><p>The complaint alleges that on O=
ctober 8, 2019, the defendant negligently operated a motor vehicle, resulti=
ng in injuries and damages to the plaintiff, who was a passenger on the bus=
. The plaintiffs claim that the defendant, as a common carrier, owed a high=
er duty of care and breached this duty, causing the injuries. The damages s=
ought include wage loss, medical expenses, and pain and suffering, among ot=
hers.</p></div><!--v-if--></div></div><div data-v-4abef554=3D""><h3 data-v-=
4abef554=3D"" id=3D"judge_bio">II. Judge Information</h3><div data-v-4abef5=
54=3D"" class=3D"my-4"><p data-v-4abef554=3D""><strong data-v-4abef554=3D""=
>Presiding Judge: </strong><span data-v-4abef554=3D""></span></p></div><div=
 data-v-4abef554=3D"" class=3D"marky"><div class=3D"md-container"><p>Judge =
Serena R. Murillo serves on the Los Angeles County Superior Court, bringing=
 a wealth of experience from her 17 years as a deputy district attorney. He=
r legal career began at McNicholas and McNicholas, but she quickly transiti=
oned to the D.A.'s office, where she handled serious cases, including gang =
and domestic violence homicides, and argued in the California Supreme Court=
 and the Ninth Circuit Court of Appeal. Her background in prosecution, part=
icularly in major fraud and central trials, provides her with a keen insigh=
t into complex legal issues.</p><p>Judge Murillo is known for her fairness =
and efficiency on the bench, with a reputation for being thorough and detai=
l-oriented. Her leadership extends beyond the courtroom; she initiated educ=
ational programs like a mock trial for elementary students to foster unders=
tanding of the judicial process. This commitment to education and community=
 reflects her broader judicial philosophy. As we prepare for our case, we s=
hould consider her prosecutorial background and attention to detail, ensuri=
ng our arguments are well-structured and evidence-based to align with her a=
nalytical approach.</p></div><!--v-if--></div></div><div data-v-4abef554=3D=
""><h3 data-v-4abef554=3D"" id=3D"complaint_summary">III. Complaint Summary=
</h3><!--v-if--><div data-v-4abef554=3D"" class=3D"marky"><div class=3D"md-=
container"><p>This complaint concerns a personal injury case filed in the S=
uperior Court of California, County of Los Angeles, by plaintiffs Laura Alc=
araz and Laura Elisa Rodriguez, through her guardian ad litem, Laura Alcara=
z. The defendants named are the Los Angeles County Metropolitan Transportat=
ion Authority (LACMTA), a public entity, along with unnamed defendants Does=
 1 to 50. The case, assigned number 20STCV36044, involves allegations of ne=
gligence related to a motor vehicle incident.</p><p>The factual allegations=
 center around an incident that occurred on October 8, 2019, at or near Ble=
dsoe Street and Foothill Boulevard in Los Angeles, California. The plaintif=
fs claim that the defendants, including LACMTA, negligently operated, maint=
ained, and entrusted a motor vehicle, resulting in injuries to the plaintif=
fs. Specifically, Laura Alcaraz was a passenger on a bus operated by the de=
fendants, who are described as common carriers with a higher duty of care. =
The complaint asserts that the defendants breached this duty, leading to th=
e injuries and damages sustained by the plaintiffs.</p><p>The legal claims =
include causes of action for motor vehicle negligence and general negligenc=
e. The plaintiffs allege that the defendants' negligent acts were the proxi=
mate cause of their injuries. The complaint references liability under Cali=
fornia Government Code Sections 815.2, 815.4, 820, and 835, which pertain t=
o the responsibilities and liabilities of public entities and their employe=
es. The plaintiffs seek to hold the defendants accountable for wage loss, m=
edical expenses, loss of earning capacity, and other related damages.</p></=
div><!--v-if--></div></div><div data-v-4abef554=3D""><h3 data-v-4abef554=3D=
"" id=3D"coa_breakdown">IV. Application of Law To Facts</h3><!--v-if--><div=
 data-v-4abef554=3D"" class=3D"marky"><div class=3D"md-container"><p>In the=
 case filed in the Superior Court of California, County of Los Angeles, the=
 plaintiffs, Laura Alcaraz and her daughter Laura Elisa Rodriguez, through =
her guardian ad litem, Laura Alcaraz, bring forth a complaint against the L=
os Angeles County Metropolitan Transportation Authority (LACMTA) and unname=
d defendants, Does 1 to 50. The complaint, assigned case number 20STCV36044=
, involves allegations of negligence resulting from a motor vehicle inciden=
t that occurred on October 8, 2019.</p><p>The jurisdiction for this case is=
 the state of California, and the legal standards applicable are those unde=
r California law, particularly focusing on negligence and the duties of com=
mon carriers. The complaint outlines two primary causes of action: motor ve=
hicle negligence and general negligence.</p><p>The first cause of action, m=
otor vehicle negligence, requires the plaintiff to establish that the defen=
dants owed a duty of care to the plaintiff, breached that duty through negl=
igent operation or maintenance of the vehicle, and that this breach was the=
 proximate cause of the plaintiff=E2=80=99s injuries. In this case, the pla=
intiffs allege that LACMTA, a public entity, and its employees negligently =
operated a bus, leading to the injuries of Laura Alcaraz and her daughter. =
They assert that the defendants failed to properly maintain and operate the=
 bus, thus breaching their duty of care. The complaint specifies that the i=
ncident occurred at or near Bledsoe Street and Foothill Boulevard in Los An=
geles, emphasizing the location as part of the factual basis for jurisdicti=
on and the occurrence of the negligent act.</p><p>The second cause of actio=
n, general negligence, involves similar elements: duty, breach, causation, =
and damages. Here, the plaintiffs argue that LACMTA and its employees, acti=
ng within their employment scope, negligently maintained, operated, and ent=
rusted the bus. As a common carrier, LACMTA is alleged to have owed a highe=
r duty of care to its passengers. The plaintiffs claim this higher duty was=
 breached, directly causing the injuries and damages they suffered. The com=
plaint references California Government Code Sections 815.2, 815.4, 820, an=
d 835, which impose liability on public entities and their employees for ne=
gligent acts committed in the course of employment.</p><p>The key facts sup=
porting these allegations include the claim that on October 8, 2019, the pl=
aintiffs were passengers on a bus operated by LACMTA. They allege that the =
negligence of the bus operator, an employee of LACMTA, led to their injurie=
s. The complaint asserts that the defendants=E2=80=99 failure to properly s=
ervice, repair, and maintain the bus was a proximate cause of the incident,=
 resulting in various damages, including wage loss, medical expenses, and l=
oss of earning capacity.</p><p>These allegations, framed within the context=
 of negligence and the duties of a common carrier, form the basis of the pl=
aintiffs' claims against LACMTA. The plaintiffs seek compensatory damages f=
or the injuries and losses they have allegedly suffered, emphasizing that t=
hese claims are based on the defendants=E2=80=99 breach of their duty of ca=
re. As the case proceeds, these allegations will require substantiation thr=
ough evidence and discovery to establish the defendants=E2=80=99 liability =
under California law.</p></div><!--v-if--></div></div><div data-v-4abef554=
=3D""><h3 data-v-4abef554=3D"" id=3D"relief_sought">V. Relief Sought</h3><!=
--v-if--><div data-v-4abef554=3D"" class=3D"marky"><div class=3D"md-contain=
er"><p>The plaintiffs, Laura Alcaraz and Laura Elisa Rodriguez, represented=
 by her guardian ad litem, are seeking relief from the Los Angeles County M=
etropolitan Transportation Authority (LACMTA) and unnamed defendants Does 1=
 to 50 for injuries sustained in a motor vehicle incident on October 8, 201=
9. The plaintiffs allege that LACMTA, a public entity, negligently operated=
, maintained, and entrusted a vehicle, causing injuries to Alcaraz, who was=
 a bus passenger. The plaintiffs claim that the defendants, as common carri=
ers, owed a heightened duty of care, which they breached, resulting in dama=
ges. The complaint seeks compensatory damages for wage loss, medical expens=
es, loss of earning capacity, and other related damages, asserting liabilit=
y under California Government Code Sections 815.2, 815.4, 820, and 835. The=
 plaintiffs request a jury trial and judgment for costs of suit, aiming for=
 relief deemed fair, just, and equitable by the court.</p></div><!--v-if-->=
</div></div><div data-v-4abef554=3D""><h3 data-v-4abef554=3D"" id=3D"key_fa=
cts">VI. Key Facts</h3><!--v-if--><div data-v-4abef554=3D"" class=3D"marky"=
><div class=3D"md-container"><p>On October 8, 2019, at or near the intersec=
tion of Bledsoe Street and Foothill Boulevard in Los Angeles, California, L=
aura Alcaraz and her daughter, Laura Elisa Rodriguez, were involved in an i=
ncident while riding as passengers on a bus operated by the Los Angeles Cou=
nty Metropolitan Transportation Authority (LACMTA). The bus, operated by an=
 employee of LACMTA, was allegedly negligently maintained, operated, and en=
trusted by LACMTA and its employees.</p><p>The complaint alleges that the d=
efendants, including LACMTA and unnamed individuals referred to as Does 1 t=
o 50, failed in their responsibilities as operators and maintainers of the =
bus. As a result, the plaintiffs claim they suffered injuries due to the ne=
gligent actions of the defendants. The plaintiffs assert that the defendant=
s, being common carriers, owed a higher duty of care to the passengers and =
breached this duty, leading to the injuries and damages sustained by Laura =
Alcaraz and her daughter. These injuries are claimed to have resulted direc=
tly from the defendants' negligence in operating and maintaining the vehicl=
e.</p></div><!--v-if--></div></div><div data-v-4abef554=3D""><h3 data-v-4ab=
ef554=3D"" id=3D"key_dates">VII. Key Dates</h3><!--v-if--><div data-v-4abef=
554=3D"" class=3D"marky"><div class=3D"md-container"><p>In the legal compla=
int filed by Laura Alcaraz and her daughter, Laura Elisa Rodriguez, against=
 the Los Angeles County Metropolitan Transportation Authority (LACMTA), sev=
eral key dates and events are highlighted, forming the foundation of their =
case. </p><p>The incident at the heart of this complaint occurred on Octobe=
r 8, 2019. On this date, the plaintiffs allege that they were passengers on=
 a bus operated by LACMTA at or near Bledsoe Street and Foothill Boulevard =
in Los Angeles, California. It is claimed that the defendants, including LA=
CMTA and its employees, negligently operated and maintained the vehicle. Th=
is negligence is asserted to have directly caused injuries to the plaintiff=
s, who argue that LACMTA, as a common carrier, had a heightened duty of car=
e which was breached on this occasion.</p><p>The formal filing of the compl=
aint took place on September 21, 2020, in the Superior Court of California,=
 County of Los Angeles. This filing initiates the legal proceedings against=
 LACMTA and unnamed defendants, Does 1 to 50, under case number 20STCV36044=
. The plaintiffs seek to hold the defendants accountable for various damage=
s, including medical expenses and loss of earning capacity, resulting from =
the alleged negligence on October 8, 2019.</p><p>These dates are significan=
t as they establish the timeline of events leading to the legal action and =
frame the context within which the plaintiffs' claims are made. The complai=
nt seeks to prove that the defendants' actions on that specific day were th=
e proximate cause of the injuries sustained, thus justifying their claims f=
or compensation.</p></div><!--v-if--></div></div><div data-v-4abef554=3D"">=
<h3 data-v-4abef554=3D"" id=3D"key_witnesses">VIII. Key Witnesses</h3><!--v=
-if--><div data-v-4abef554=3D"" class=3D"marky"><div class=3D"md-container"=
><p>In the context of the ongoing litigation involving Laura Alcaraz and he=
r daughter, Laura Elisa Rodriguez, against the Los Angeles County Metropoli=
tan Transportation Authority (LACMTA), identifying the key witnesses and pa=
rties is crucial to understanding the dynamics of the case and guiding the =
discovery process.</p><p>At the heart of this litigation are the plaintiffs=
, Laura Alcaraz and her daughter, who were passengers on a bus operated by =
the LACMTA at the time of the incident on October 8, 2019. Laura Alcaraz, a=
cting individually and as the guardian ad litem for her daughter, claims th=
at the negligence of the LACMTA and its employees resulted in significant p=
ersonal injuries. The plaintiffs assert that the LACMTA, as a common carrie=
r, owed them a heightened duty of care, which they allegedly breached, lead=
ing to the injuries suffered.</p><p>The primary defendant in this case is t=
he Los Angeles County Metropolitan Transportation Authority, a public entit=
y responsible for the operation and maintenance of the bus involved in the =
incident. The complaint also names numerous unidentified individuals, refer=
red to as Does 1 to 50, who are alleged to be employees or agents of the LA=
CMTA. These individuals are believed to have played a role in the negligent=
